@startuml PasstheKeys
title PasstheKeys - frontend nonce-hijack: the attacker ends up inside the victim's account on the SAME website

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName sans-serif
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam ParticipantPadding 16
skinparam BoxPadding 12

actor "Victim" as V
box "Acme Workspace - one website, one origin" #EEF1FF
  participant "Victim's tab\n/login (backdoored)" as VP
  participant "Attacker's tab\n/attacker" as AP
end box
participant "Dead-drop\n(/x/offer, /x/assertion)" as S
participant "Pocket ID\n(via same-origin /api)" as PID
actor "Attacker" as A

note over VP, AP
  Both people are on the SAME website (Acme Workspace, one origin). The Pocket ID login
  ceremony runs in the ATTACKER's tab; the server is only a dead-drop. No token is ever
  read - the attacker finishes the login itself, so the session lands in its own browser.
end note

== Attacker arms, in its own tab of the site ==
A -> AP : open Acme Workspace
AP -> PID : GET /api/webauthn/login/start
PID --> AP : nonce **Ca** + session cookie (stays in the attacker's tab)
AP -> S : POST /x/offer { Ca }   (publish the nonce, do NOT finish)

== Victim tries to log into the site; signs the attacker's nonce ==
V -> VP : click "Sign in"
VP -> S : GET /x/offer -> **Ca**   (picks up the attacker's nonce, not its own)
VP -> V : navigator.credentials.get({ challenge: **Ca** })
V --> VP : assertion over **Ca** (victim's key, this origin, user verified)
VP -> S : POST /x/assertion { assertion }
VP -> V : "Hmm, that did not work" - victim is NOT logged into Acme Workspace

== Attacker finishes the login and is now inside the victim's account ==
AP -> S : GET /x/assertion -> assertion
AP -> PID : POST /api/webauthn/login/finish (assertion + its own session cookie)
PID --> AP : 200 + Set-Cookie **access_token** (the victim's session, in the attacker's tab)
AP -> PID : GET /api/users/me
PID --> AP : the victim's profile
AP -> A : Acme Workspace home: "Welcome back, <victim>"

note over V, A #FFF3E0
  Same website, opposite ends: the victim is locked out of their own Acme Workspace account;
  the attacker's tab of Acme Workspace is signed in AS the victim.
  Why it works: the victim's signature is valid for THIS origin and the attacker is on this
  origin, so the attacker can finish a ceremony it started. The passkey checks the origin and
  the signature; it never asks who the nonce belonged to.
  Where Tide closes it:
    Going in   - subresource integrity: the swapped-in script never loads, so none of this runs.
    Coming out - DPoP / sender-constrained token: bound to the device, useless in another browser.
end note

@enduml
